## Bell Numbers: Counting Set Partitions
### Overview

Bell numbers are a sequence of numbers that appear in combinatorial mathematics, beginning with `B0 = 1`, and using the recurrence relation to find subsequent numbers. They count the number of partitions of a set.

### Formula

The recurrence relation for Bell numbers is given by:
`B(n+1) = Σ(S(n, k))` from `k=0` to `n`, where `S(n, k)` are Stirling numbers of the second kind.

### Java Function

Here is a Java function to calculate Bell numbers:

```java
public class BellNumbers {

    /**
     * Calculate the nth Bell number using dynamic programming.
     *
     * @param n the index of the Bell number to calculate
     * @return the nth Bell number
     */
    public static long bellNumber(int n) {
        long[] bell = new long[n + 1];
        bell[0] = 1;

        // Fill entries so that they sum up to bell(i)
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            // Fill so that they sum up to bell(i)
            bell[i] = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                bell[i] += binomialCoefficient(i - 1, j) * bell[j];
            }
        }

        return bell[n];
    }

    /**
     * Calculate the binomial coefficient (n choose k).
     *
     * @param n the total number of items
     * @param k the number of items to choose
     * @return the binomial coefficient (n choose k)
     */
    private static long binomialCoefficient(int n, int k) {
        if (k > n - k) {
            k = n - k;
        }

        long result = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            result = result * (n - i) / (i + 1);
        }

        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 5;
        System.out.println("B(" + n + ") = " + bellNumber(n));
    }
}
```

